    "content": "The Artemis II Orion capsule successfully splashed down in the Pacific Ocean off the coast of San Diego, California, on Friday, April 10, 2026, at 8:07 p.m. EDT, marking the end of a 10-day crewed lunar mission. The capsule used a multi-stage parachute system to slow its descent to approximately 20 mph after re-entering Earth’s atmosphere.Artemis II Landing Highlights:Splashdown Location: Pacific Ocean near San Diego, California.Mission Duration: Approximately 10 days, with the crew splashing down after a trip around the Moon.Reentry Speed: The capsule hit the atmosphere at nearly 24,000 mph before employing its heat shield and parachutes.Crew Recovery: NASA and US Navy teams recovered the four astronauts via helicopter to the USS John P. Murtha.Key Aspects of Space Capsule Landings:Heat Shield & Parachutes: Essential components that survive high temperatures (5,000∘F+) and slow the capsule from orbital speeds to a safe landing speed.Splashdown vs. Land Landing: Unlike Russian Soyuz capsules, which land on land, American capsules like Orion and SpaceX Crew Dragon use water-based splashdowns for safety, preferring calm, predictable waters.Reusability: SpaceX Crew Dragon capsules are frequently reused to reduce costs, often splashing down in the Atlantic Ocean or Gulf of Mexico.The Artemis II mission serves as a key precursor to future Moon landings, verifying that the spacecraft is safe for humans.",
